---
import ArticleLayout from '../../components/ArticleLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'When should you replace an HVAC system instead of repairing it?',
    a: 'Replace when the system is past about 10 to 12 years and a repair would cost more than a third of a new system, or when it needs its second major repair in two years. A common rule: if the repair cost times the unit’s age is more than about 5,000, replace it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it worth repairing an old water heater?',
    a: 'A tank water heater past 10 years that is leaking from the tank itself is not worth repairing, replace it. Repairs make sense for younger units or for fixable parts like a heating element, thermostat, or valve.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you know if a roof needs replacing or just repair?',
    a: 'Isolated storm damage or a single leak is usually a repair. Replace when shingles are curling and losing granules across the whole roof, the roof is past 20 years, or repairs are becoming a yearly event.',
  },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  slug="repair-or-replace-hvac-roof-water-heater"
  eyebrow="Decision Guide"
  title="Repair or Replace? A Cost Guide for HVAC, Roofs, and Water Heaters"
  description="When it is worth repairing a major system and when replacement is the smarter money, with the age and cost thresholds that make the call for you."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    The repair-or-replace call comes down to two numbers: <strong>the age of
    the system and the cost of the repair relative to a new one.</strong>
    Repair a young system with a cheap fix. Replace an old system facing an
    expensive one. The gray zone in between is where most owners overspend,
    either sinking money into a dying unit or replacing something with years
    left. Here are the thresholds that make the call.
  </p>

  <h2>The universal rule</h2>
  <div class="takeaway">
    <p>
      If the repair would cost more than one-third the price of a new system,
      or the system is past the middle of its expected lifespan and needs its
      second major repair, replacement is usually the smarter money. For
      HVAC specifically, a widely used shortcut is to multiply the repair cost
      by the unit's age: if the result tops about 5,000, replace it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>HVAC</h2>
  <p>
    A central AC lasts 12 to 15 years, a furnace 15 to 20 (see
    <a href="/articles/how-long-do-home-systems-last/">full lifespans</a>).
    Repair a system under 10 years for most issues. Once it passes 12, weigh
    every repair against replacement, especially compressor or coil failures,
    which are the expensive ones. A system using older R-22 refrigerant is
    also a replace signal, since that refrigerant is phased out and costly.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Lean repair</th><th>Lean replace</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>Under 10 years</td><td>Over 12 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repair cost</td><td>Under 1/3 of new</td><td>Over 1/3 of new</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Repair history</td><td>First repair</td><td>Second major repair in 2 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refrigerant</td><td>Current (R-410A)</td><td>Old R-22</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Water heater</h2>
  <p>
    Tank heaters last 8 to 12 years. The deciding question is <strong>where
    the failure is.</strong> A bad heating element, thermostat, or valve on a
    younger unit is a cheap repair. But if the tank itself is leaking, or the
    unit is past 10 years, replace it, a repaired old tank often fails again
    within months, and a tank failure on an upper floor causes far more damage
    than the heater is worth.
  </p>

  <h2>Roof</h2>
  <p>
    A roof is the one system where repair stays viable longest, because damage
    is often local. Repair isolated storm damage, a single leak, or a small
    section of lifted shingles. Replace when granule loss and curling show up
    across the whole roof, when the roof is past 20 years, or when you are
    patching leaks every season. In Middle Tennessee, an annual inspection is
    what keeps you ahead of that call instead of behind it.
  </p>

  <h2>The landlord angle</h2>
  <p>
    On a rental, factor in tenant disruption and emergency risk, not just the
    quote. A marginal water heater you would nurse along in your own home is
    worth replacing proactively in a rental, because a failure means a tenant
    call, possible water damage, and an after-hours bill. Planned replacement
    on your schedule always beats emergency replacement on the system's.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Know the age, compare the repair to one-third of new, and count the repair
    history. Upkeep handles both sides of the call across Middle Tennessee, we
    will tell you honestly when a repair beats a replacement and when it does
    not, on <a href="/services/hvac/">HVAC</a>,
    <a href="/services/roofing/">roofing</a>, and plumbing. One
    <a href="/work-order/">service request</a> gets you a straight answer.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
